WAITFILE is a multitasker-aware utility which waits until a file is either
created, or optionally waits for one to be removed, giving up time slices
while it waits.

WAITFIL2 is an OS/2-specific version of WAITFILE which does not use up any
cpu cycles during the 5 second wait in between each test for the existence of
the file.

usage:  WAITFILE filename seconds (remove)

  WAITFILE checks for the existence of 'filename' once every five seconds
  and continues to retry the operation for as many 'seconds' as specified.

  The 'remove' keyword is optional and, if used, indicates that WAITFILE is
  waiting for 'filename' to be removed instead of created

  When the confirming action occurs (when the filename is created, or if
  "removed" is specified, when the file is removed), then WAITFILE exits
  with an errorlevel of 1.

  After "seconds" expires, WAITFILE exits when an errorlevel of 0.


Example usage inside of a batch file:

  :wait
    waitfile c:\update.now 30
    if errorlevel 1 goto process
    rem we could do something else here which would happen once every
    rem 30 seconds due to the "30" on the waitfile command line
    goto wait

  :process
    rem finish processing

